 "cover": "Captives, debtors and the ports that wanted them: four centuries of forced movement.",
 "intro": "For four centuries people were raided, sold, pledged for debt and shipped across the waters of the archipelago. Some stayed near home; some were carried past the edge of this map to Ceylon, the Cape or the Mascarene islands. This story follows the routes historians have been able to reconstruct, says where their numbers are guesses, and tries to remember that every line on the map was a person.",
 "slides": [
  {
   "id": "s01",
   "label": "Early 1500s",
   "title": "Bondage before the trade",
   "text": "Anthony Reid argued that slaves were everywhere in the great trading cities. Remco Raben re-reads the thin evidence, mostly Tomé Pires in the 1510s, and finds the numbers modest. Melaka's private slaves on the eve of 1511 were a few thousand, probably under a tenth of the city. Ten to twelve Palembang junks a year carried people alongside rice.",
   "more": "Raben stresses that bondage took many forms: debt, punishment, war capture and trade, all blurring into each other. Most bonded labourers worked close to where they were born. Long-distance traffic existed, but it fed courts and elites more than it filled whole cities.",
