   "label": "Mid-1500s–1610s",
   "title": "Captives raised the forts",
   "text": "Raben describes mass enslavement as a tool of ambitious rulers rather than of ordinary commerce. In mid-sixteenth-century South Sulawesi, Gowa's ruler Tunipalangga set captives to building his forts. In the 1610s Aceh's Iskandar Muda deported thousands of men from wars on the Malay peninsula. Raben calls such hunger for manpower exceptional, and notes it needed raiding, not purchase.",
